g":null,"pf":[],"description_full":"Lamb Weston Holdings, Inc. engages in the production, distribution, and marketing of frozen potato products in the United States, Canada, Mexico, and internationally. The company offers frozen potatoes, commercial ingredients, and appetizers under the Lamb Weston brand, as well as under various customer labels. It also provides its products under its owned or licensed brands, such as Grown in Idaho and Alexia, and other licensed brands, as well as under retailers' own brands. It sells its products through a network of internal sales personnel and independent brokers, agents, and distributors to quick service and full-service restaurants and chains, wholesale, grocery, mass merchants, club retailers, non-commercial channels, and specialty retailers, as well as foodservice distributors and institutions, including businesses, educational institutions, independent restaurants, regional chain restaurants, and convenience stores. Lamb Weston Holdings, Inc. was incorporated in 1950 and is headquartered in Eagle, Idaho.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Michael Jared Smith | President, CEO & Director | Age 48 | Compensation: $2.9M (FY2026)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 10K people. Revenue per employee of $661K ranks in the above the sector median among Consumer Defensive peers, with revenue growing 9%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $54K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $5K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 4\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (3/10).","financial_summary":["Lamb Weston Holdings, Inc. is a mid-cap consumer defensive business with a $7.7B market cap on $6.6B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +7% over the past three years, a respectable pace for a business of this size.","Returns on capital run at 13% against a 7% cost of capital, a 6-point spread that creates value, though narrow enough that competitive pressure could close it, and 24% gross margins are typical of commodity economics where competitors can replicate the product cheaply.","The business throws off $540M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 8 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 3.25\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, debt is moderate at 3.8\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, serviceable in normal conditions but worth watching through a downturn.","Shareholder yield of 2.7% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insiders hold 2.7% of the shares."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 456% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away (confirmed by 1 other valuation measure)","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":4.557051668235928},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Altman Z in the grey zone \u2014 financial health warrants monitoring","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 7.0% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Slow-motion taste and channel shifts \u2014 private label, Gen-Z health preferences, or a DTC disruption can erode decade-old brand moats without a single dramatic quarter.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Input cost inflation from agricultural commodities \u2014 when raw materials spike, pass-through pricing lags and margin compression is immediate.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"GLP-1 and health-trend demand destruction \u2014 weight-loss drugs and wellness shifts are structurally reducing consumption in snack, beverage, and packaged food categories.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Large open-market insider purc